nts/story/validate.test.ts\ncomponents/story/faces.ts\ncomponents/story/use-reduced-motion.ts\ncomponents/story/guard-extra-callback.ts\ncomponents/story/look-switch.tsx\ncomponents/story/stage.tsx\ncomponents/story/use-look.ts\ncomponents/story/use-webgl-supported.ts\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /var/tmp/storyfolio-comparison-runtime-20261012/storyfolio-evals/build-bench-depth-2-tpXd60/.agents/skills/storyfolio/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /var/tmp/storyfolio-comparison-runtime-20261012/storyfolio-evals/build-bench-depth-2-tpXd60/.agents/skills/storyfolio/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: storyfolio\ndescription: Use when someone wants a personal portfolio site made from their profile, resume, LinkedIn or GitHub, especially one with 3D, scroll-driven animation or a story, or says their portfolio looks generic, templated or AI-made.\n---\n\n# Storyfolio\n\nMakes one kind of portfolio site: one long scroll that tells one story, carried by one object in 3D that is taken from the person's own work.\n\nThis version plans the site and does not build it. It ends when the person has said yes to a storyboard.\n\n## Three stops\n\nBetween stops you work by yourself. At each stop the person decides one thing. You go on only when their answer is in the conversation and you have written it into the file.\n\n| Stop | First open | You put to the person | You go on when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Read | `references/reading-a-profile.md` | What you found, as facts to confirm and questions to answer. Nothing else | They have answered, and `docs/story/facts.md` holds their answers |\n| 2. Concept | `references/concept.md` | Three concepts, and which you would choose | They have picked one, or told you to pick, and `docs/story/concepts.md` says which |\n| 3. Storyboard | `references/storyboard.md` | The script of the whole page | They have said yes to it |\n\n**Your first reply is the first stop, and no more than that.** People ask for the whole plan at once: the idea, the scroll, the colours. Do not give it yet, and say why in a sentence. A plan made now is made from your reading of a resume, and a resume leaves out exactly what a site must not get wrong: whose result a number was, what a title hides, who took a photograph. Once they have answered you will have a better idea, and it will not have to be taken apart.\n\nWhen the storyboard has its yes, say that building is not in this version, and bring `docs/story/handover.md` up to date.\n\n## Ask or decide\n\nAsk the person when a thing is:\n\n1. a fact only they know,\n2. a claim that could embarrass them if it were wrong,\n3. taste on what a visitor sees first: the concept, the look, the opening words,\n4. anything that publishes, costs money, or shows another person or an employer's work.\n\nEverything else you decide. Write each decision into `docs/story/decided-for-you.md`: what you decided, why, and how to change it.\n\nIf the person will not answer, or tells you to decide everything, open `references/asking.md`.\n\n## What you keep\n\nIn the person's own folder, so that a later session can go on from the files alone:\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `docs/story/facts.md` | The fact sheet, each fact with where it came from, and the person's answers |\n| `docs/story/concepts.md` | The three concepts, which was picked, and why |\n| `docs/story/storyboard.md` | The script |\n| `docs/story/decided-for-you.md` | What you decided without asking |\n| `docs/story/handover.md` | What exists, what the person has said, what is next |\n\n## What holds from the first stop on\n\n- **Nothing about the person is invented.** You know that a number, an employer, a title and a quote are facts. So is every small thing a sentence says on its way: what was wrong before they came, how often a thing is used, which came first, why, how a thing was left, what it taught them. That sounds like writing, and it is a claim. Before handing over any reply or file, check each claim against the fact sheet, including introductory summaries, working titles and labels on the object's faces. Read the supporting line and its unresolved questions, not just its reference. It must establish the whole claim: a topic attached to one item does not establish the topic of another. Wording that depends on an unanswered question stays out of the introduction and the planned page, even if copied from a profile. Remove it or list it as a question to confirm.\n- **A number is used as the person confirmed it,** with what it measures and whose result it is.\n- **What is someone else's is asked about before it is planned for:** a photograph a photographer took, a friend's drawing, a quote, an employer's screens, a story with a patient or a client in it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
