      "text": "<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charSet="utf-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"/><link rel="preload" href="/assets/power-states.svg" as="image"/><link rel="preload" href="/assets/bring-up-fixture.svg" as="image"/><link rel="stylesheet" href="/_next/static/chunks/43sl-gui1lqxe.css" data-precedence="next"/><link rel="preload" as="script" fetchPriority="low" href="/_next/static/chunks/09lgyggnhjlha.js"/><script src="/_next/static/chunks/00lbe-vqlafyt.js" async=""></script><script src="/_next/static/chunks/1rj7ns8rte9vc.js" async=""></script><script src="/_next/static/chunks/turbopack-3zdm2e7s4tp9w.js" async=""></script><script src="/_next/static/chunks/3fntmmi971322.js" async=""></script><script src="/_next/static/chunks/0xln3ym2u8rvk.js" async=""></script><title>Ada Mensah · Code to bench</title><meta name="description" content="An illustrative five-beat prototype about embedded firmware, bench checks and the limits of evidence."/><script src="/_next/static/chunks/0cz1d0mv5g_q7.js" noModule=""></script></head><body><div hidden=""><!--$--><!--/$--></div><main><a id="story-skip" class="story-skip" href="#chapter-scope">Skip to the answer</a><div class="story" data-scene="off" data-look="bench" style="--story-ground:#e8e7df;--story-ink:#192d30;--story-faint:#475b5b;--story-accent:#96502b"><noscript><style>.story-look-switch, .story-control { display: none !important; }</style></noscript><div class="story-content"><header class="bench-masthead"><span>ADA MENSAH</span><span>CODE / BENCH</span><span>LOCAL PROTOTYPE · 01</span></header><section id="chapter-question" aria-labelledby="chapter-question-title"><div data-beat="question" class="story-beat" style="--beat-height:1.6"><div class="story-caption"><div class="story-caption-text"><p aria-hidden="true" class="story-kicker"><span>1</span><span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>Code → physical check</p><h1 id="chapter-question-title" class="story-title">How does code become a physical check?</h1><p class="story-body">Ada Mensah works with embedded firmware: code that controls a physical device. For Mosslink, an environmental sensor, she wrote the firmware, scheduled its power states and built the bring-up fixture.</p><p class="story-note">Bring-up means checking a new board’s basic operation. Follow the apparatus to see how a code decision connects to something observable on the bench.</p><p class="apparatus-key">Illustrative apparatus · fictional subject and sensor. This is an explanatory model, not an actual Mosslink device.</p></div></div></div></section><section id="chapter-scheduling" aria-labelledby="chapter-scheduling-title"><div data-beat="scheduling" class="story-beat" style="--beat-height:1.6"><div class="story-caption"><div class="story-caption-text"><p aria-hidden="true" class="story-kicker"><span>2</span><span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>Organize a cycle</p><h2 id="chapter-scheduling-title" class="story-title">Give each activity its place.</h2><p class="story-body">Sleep → Measure → Transmit → repeat. This teaching loop shows how scheduling organizes different activities into a cycle. It gives a reader an order of behavior to inspect when code meets a board.</p><p class="story-note">Ada scheduled power states. This simplified sequence is illustrative, not recovered Mosslink firmware or measured timing. No durations or current values are supplied.</p><p class="apparatus-key">The three colored towers mark the teaching sequence; their spacing and heights carry no timing or current data.</p><div class="story-fallback"><img src="/assets/power-states.svg" width="720" height="620" alt="Illustrative Sleep, Measure, Transmit teaching loop, not actual firmware or measured timing."/></div></div></div></div></section><section id="chapter-bench" aria-labelledby="chapter-bench-title"><div data-beat="bench" class="story-beat" style="--beat-height:1.6"><div class="story-caption"><div class="story-caption-text"><p aria-hidden="true" class="story-kicker"><span>3</span><span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>Open the bench</p><h2 id="chapter-bench-title" class="story-title">Make the board accessible.</h2><p class="story-body">A firmware command asks the physical board to act. The bring-up fixture provides access so that its response can be observed on the bench. The fixture helps check behavior; the board’s circuitry still performs the device’s work.</p><p class="story-note">Ada: firmware, power-state scheduling and bring-up fixture. A colleague: the PCB (printed circuit board) and analogue front end, the circuitry interfacing with sensor signals.</p><p class="apparatus-key">The opened model explains command → board response → fixture access → bench observation. It is not a wiring plan or a public debugging incident.</p><div class="story-fallback"><img src="/assets/bring-up-fixture.svg" width="720" height="620" alt="Illustrative relationship between firmware command, board, fixture access and bench observation, not a wiring plan."/></div></div></div></div></section><section id="chapter-evidence" aria-labelledby="chapter-evidence-title"><div data-beat="evidence" class="story-beat" style="--beat-height:1.8"><div class="story-caption"><div class="story-caption-text"><p aria-hidden="true" class="story-kicker"><span>4</span><span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>Read the evidence</p><h2 id="chapter-evidence-title" class="story-title">Two claims. Two kinds of support.</h2><p class="story-body">Measured sleep and transmit current, together with an assumed reporting interval, support a two-year endurance projection. Separately, the longest continuous bench run was 72 hours: an observed duration.</p><p class="story-note">The 72-hour run does not validate two years of endurance. Neither path establishes field conditions or customer deployment. No original measurements, current trace, spreadsheet or run notebook is supplied.</p><p class="apparatus-key">Left: measured inputs + assumed interval → projection. Right: continuous bench run → observation. The display labels are explanatory, not recovered instrument readings.</p></div></div></div></section><section id="chapter-scope" aria-labelledby="chapter-scope-title"><div data-beat="scope" class="story-beat" style="--beat-height:1.6"><div class="story-caption"><div class="story-caption-text"><p aria-hidden="true" class="story-kicker"><span>5</span><span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>Bound the answer</p><h2 id="chapter-scope-title" class="story-title">A connection you can check. A claim with limits.</h2><p class="story-body">Ada’s firmware and scheduling decisions connect to an observable bench check through the fixture she built. That is the contribution this story can support—not ownership of the whole sensor.</p><p class="story-note">Forty engineering samples were assembled; six were bench-tested; none went to customers. One preliminary emissions check passed. Full qualification is unfinished.</p><p class="apparatus-key">State tables, timing diagrams, current traces and bench checklists are working documents. None is supplied here as original evidence. This fictional local prototype is ready for review, not publication.</p></div></div></div></section></div></div></main><!--$--><!--/$--><script src="/_next/static/chunks/09lgyggnhjlha.js" id="_R_" async=""></script><script>(self.__next_f=self.__next_f||[]).push([0])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"1:\"$Sreact.fragment\"\n2:I[39756,[\"/_next/static/chunks/3fntmmi971322.js\"],\"default\"]\n3:I[37457,[\"/_next/static/chunks/3fntmmi971322.js\"],\"default\"]\n4:I[62123,[\"/_next/static/chunks/3fntmmi971322.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/0xln3ym2u8rvk.js\"],\"Experience\"]\nd:I[68027,[\"/_next/static/chunks/3fntmmi971322.js\"],\"default\",1]\n:HL[\"/_next/static/chunks/43sl-gui1lqxe.css\",\"style\"]\n0:{\"P\":null,\"c\":[\"\",\"\"],\"q\":\"\",\"i\":false,\"f\":[[[\"\",{\"children\":[\"__PAGE__\",{},\"$undefined\",\"$undefined\",4608]},\"$undefined\",\"$undefined\",4624],[[\"$\",\"